# EmComm GPS receiver binding — copy to configs/gps.conf and edit.
#
# Per-unit hardware, so configs/gps.conf is gitignored: a by-id path carries
# the receiver's serial number, which is machine data and must not land in the
# repository. This .sample ships; your real file does not — configs/*.conf is
# already gitignored here, as everything in configs/ is.
#
# DEVICE
#   Use a /dev/serial/by-id/ path, NOT /dev/ttyUSB0. Device numbering moves
#   the moment another serial device is attached — a radio CAT interface will
#   take ttyUSB0 out from under the GPS. The by-id path is keyed on vendor,
#   product and serial number, so it survives that.
#
#   Find it with the receiver attached:
#       ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/
#
#   Note: a by-id entry only exists if the device reports a serial number.
#   Most do; some cheap adapters do not, and those have no stable path at all.
#
# BAUD
#   Optional. Omit it and gpsd hunts for the speed, which can take longer than
#   it is willing to wait on a slow receiver. Set it if you know it — most
#   NMEA pucks are 4800 or 9600.
#
# The provisioner never probes for a receiver. Opening a serial port asserts
# DTR and some CAT interfaces key PTT on DTR or RTS, so a sweep of ttyUSB*
# looking for a GPS could put a radio on the air. You name the device; the
# provisioner touches nothing else.
#
# Verified example — GlobalSat BU-353N, NMEA 4800 8N1:
#
# DEVICE=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Prolific_Technology_Inc._USB-Serial_Controller_EXAMPLE0000-if00-port0
# BAUD=4800

DEVICE=
BAUD=
